## Proof

We compute
$$L = \lim_{x\to\infty}\frac{1}{x}\int_{x/3}^{3x} g(t)\,dt.$$

**Key observation.** Since $\lim_{t\to\infty}g(t)=\pi$, for every $\varepsilon>0$ there exists $M>0$ such that
$$t\ge M \implies |g(t)-\pi|<\varepsilon.$$

Choose $x$ large enough that $x/3 \ge M$ (i.e. $x\ge 3M$). Then the entire interval $[x/3,\,3x]$ lies in $\{t\ge M\}$, so $|g(t)-\pi|<\varepsilon$ for all $t\in[x/3,3x]$.

**Decomposition.** Write
$$\int_{x/3}^{3x}g(t)\,dt = \int_{x/3}^{3x}\pi\,dt + \int_{x/3}^{3x}\bigl(g(t)-\pi\bigr)\,dt.$$

The first term is exact:
$$\int_{x/3}^{3x}\pi\,dt = \pi\bigl(3x - \tfrac{x}{3}\bigr) = \pi\cdot\frac{8x}{3}.$$

For the second term, using the uniform bound on $[x/3,3x]$:
$$\left|\int_{x/3}^{3x}\bigl(g(t)-\pi\bigr)\,dt\right| \le \int_{x/3}^{3x}|g(t)-\pi|\,dt < \varepsilon\cdot\bigl(3x-\tfrac{x}{3}\bigr)=\varepsilon\cdot\frac{8x}{3}.$$

**Divide by $x$.** For all $x\ge 3M$,
$$\frac{1}{x}\int_{x/3}^{3x}g(t)\,dt = \frac{8\pi}{3} + \frac{1}{x}\int_{x/3}^{3x}\bigl(g(t)-\pi\bigr)\,dt,$$
and the remainder satisfies
$$\left|\frac{1}{x}\int_{x/3}^{3x}\bigl(g(t)-\pi\bigr)\,dt\right| < \frac{1}{x}\cdot\varepsilon\cdot\frac{8x}{3}=\frac{8\varepsilon}{3}.$$

**Conclusion.** For every $\varepsilon>0$, there exists $X=3M$ such that for all $x\ge X$,
$$\left|\frac{1}{x}\int_{x/3}^{3x}g(t)\,dt - \frac{8\pi}{3}\right|<\frac{8\varepsilon}{3}.$$

Since $\varepsilon>0$ is arbitrary, by the definition of a limit,
$$\boxed{L=\frac{8\pi}{3}}.$$

**Remark on the hypotheses.** The unboundedness of $g$ plays no role: it only concerns the behavior of $g$ near $0$ (or on bounded subintervals of $(0,\infty)$), whereas the integration domain $[x/3,3x]$ escapes to $+\infty$ as $x\to\infty$. Continuity guarantees the integral is well defined on every compact subinterval, and the limit at infinity alone controls the asymptotic value. The ratio $\frac{8}{3}$ is precisely the ratio of the interval length $3x-x/3=\frac{8x}{3}$ to the scaling factor $x$.

